Registry fixtures for section A of docs/plans/v0.1.md (A2, A3), and for the tests of sections C and D
that read a registry. Each .tsv is a whole registry in format 1 unless it says otherwise. They are
written by hand, with fixed ids that match A2's patterns; no id here is ever minted again.

The ids
  rmmsi4vdvxsr4  the household recipe          rsvh4l2c3prht  fit-test, a second recipe (fit.tsv)
  ebv74racygnzm  engine b12359                  e3umyg6om6bfo  engine b12400 (made up, good-v0.2.0)
  mjhs5h65av6o4  Mistral Small 4 (endorsed)     maw6njwkqcc35  Magistral Small 2509 (publisher)
  mwod7poxly5a4  a third-party model            mhao5xfx4uaxi, mxxcb4456zr2v  the model stand-ins A
  b7275bkg6tizv  order 1 (b12359 + Mistral)       and B of tests/helpers.sh model_standins
  b7adwcqnfap56  order 2 (b12359 + Magistral)   bv4tbuqiaa3al  Mistral on b12400 (good-v0.2.0)
  b4cvp4l4ee5tl  the third-party build          b5gekhbuvd6hx, bc35hcwnetoqb  fits / does not fit
  bhoe4j755eetl  a build in no order line       bqcj2a3zgsijq  never defined (withdraw-unknown)

The household lines follow A6: real sizes, hashes and commits for the engine and both models, the
system prompt c44bab6b...d02e49 and order 2's template 39f03c38...81fdcda. Two values are placeholders,
because the files do not exist yet: order 1's template (d54dd73b...) and the recipe's test (41bb822f...).
The model context limits (262,144 and 131,072), order 1's top-p (0.95) and the Mac label dev-mac-64gb
are fixture values.

What a correct reader does with each file

good-v0.1.0.tsv      Uses every line: one recipe, order 1 then order 2.
good-v0.2.0.tsv      The same lines byte for byte, the version v0.2.0, and three added: engine b12400, a
                     build of Mistral on it, and a later order line that puts that build first. The two
                     are the registry at two versions, the later one appended to.
withdrawn.tsv        good-v0.1.0 and `withdraw` of order 1 (security): order 1 is withdrawn, order 2 is
                     chosen.
tier.tsv             good-v0.1.0, a third-party model and its build, put first by a later order line:
                     the recipe allows publisher,endorsed, so that build is never chosen; order 1 is.
fit.tsv              Recipe fit-test, one person at 4,096 to 8,192. Its order puts first a build that
                     fits no Mac (1,000,000 MiB of weights, 4,000,000 bytes of cache a token) and second
                     one that fits any (2,000 MiB). The models are the stand-ins of tests/helpers.sh.
malformed-line.tsv   good-v0.1.0 and a later order line whose last build id ends in a space: that line
                     is passed over, so the earlier order stands and order 1 is chosen.
hostile.tsv          good-v0.1.0 and five model or engine lines that nothing names: a repository
                     ../mistralai, a file name -rf.gguf, a file name $(touch f).gguf, a sha256 with z,
                     a size 17e6. The last four are passed over, the recipe is used, and no file f is
                     made. See the open questions about the first.
dup-ids.tsv          good-v0.1.0, then a second `model` line for Magistral's id (one byte larger) and a
                     second `build` line for order 1's id (other figures). The gate fails it and names
                     both. A Mac keeps the first definition of each and passes over the second (T48).
line-1000.tsv        good-v0.1.0 and a comment line of exactly 1,000 bytes, not counting its newline:
                     within the cap; the gate passes it.
line-1001.tsv        The same with 1,001 bytes: the gate fails it, naming line 9. A Mac passes over that
                     line and uses the rest, so it reads as line-1000.tsv does.
unknown-kind.tsv     good-v0.1.0 and a line of the kind `tool`: passed over, the rest used (T40).
field-count.tsv      good-v0.1.0, a `recipe` line one field short and a `withdraw` line one field long:
                     both passed over, so the recipe keeps its first line and order 1 is not withdrawn.
empty-middle.tsv     good-v0.1.0 and a later `recipe` line with an empty third field, its values moved
                     one field right (family-helper, publisher, 2 people, 8,192 to 16,384). Split on
                     single tabs it has 8 fields and is passed over: the recipe stays household,
                     publisher,endorsed, and order 1 is chosen. A reader that splits on runs of tabs
                     reads it as 7 good fields and chooses order 2 (T50).
crlf-line.tsv        good-v0.1.0 and a later order line ending in CR LF: passed over, order 1 chosen.
crlf-all.tsv         good-v0.1.0 with every line ending in CR LF. The first line's version field is
                     "v0.1.0" and a CR, and every record's last field carries a CR: nothing is used.
format-2.tsv         The first line says format 2: not read at all; every recipe shows
                     `passed over (cannot be read)` (T40).
judgements.tsv       good-v0.1.0, a third build that is in no order line, a second `recipe` line
                     (family-helper, 2 people, 8,192 to 16,384) and a second `order` line (order 2,
                     then order 1). The later recipe and order lines are used, both earlier ones stay
                     in the file, order 2 is chosen, and the build in no order line never is (T51).
withdraw-unknown.tsv good-v0.1.0, a `withdraw` of a build id defined nowhere, and a `withdraw` naming
                     Magistral's model id: the gate fails both; a Mac passes both over and withdraws
                     nothing.
reasons.tsv          good-v0.1.0, `withdraw` order 1 `stolen` (not a known reason, but it matches the
                     pattern: order 1 is withdrawn and shows only "withdrawn"), and two `withdraw` lines
                     for order 2 with the reasons `Security` and an escape sequence: both fail the
                     pattern and are passed over, so order 2 stays offered and is chosen (T21).

Made at run time, not kept here: tests/helpers.sh registry_sized SRC BYTES OUT pads a registry with
comment lines of at most 1,000 bytes to an exact size. good-v0.1.0.tsv padded to 524,288 bytes is
within the cap; to 524,289 it is over (T48); to 1,048,577 it is an answer over 1 MiB (T3).

boundary/            For every pattern of A2 and A3, values just inside and just outside (T49).
                     MANIFEST lists each file, the file it differs from, the lines a correct reader
                     passes over, and what it tests. in-upper.tsv holds every field at its largest or
                     longest value, in-lower.tsv at its smallest or shortest; both are used whole, and
                     their one build is chosen. Each out-*.tsv is in-upper.tsv (or in-withdraw.tsv)
                     with one value changed, everywhere that value appears, so the lines it differs on
                     are exactly the lines MANIFEST names; tests/t00-fixtures.sh checks that. Each line
                     named fails a pattern itself. With the only build or its order gone, the recipe
                     shows `passed over (cannot be read)`. The out-reason files are the exception: the
                     withdraw line is passed over, so the build stays offered.

Open questions in the plan that these fixtures meet, not settled here
- A3's repository pattern accepts `..` as the owner or the name (hostile.tsv line 9 matches it), while
  T22 expects a line holding `../` to be passed over. A Mac builds a download address from it.
- A line that names an id whose own line was passed over (an order naming a build whose build line
  failed a pattern): T48 says a line naming an undefined id is passed over, but not whether an id
  defined by a line that was passed over counts as undefined. The boundary files do not depend on it:
  either way the recipe cannot be read.
- `order` has no fixed field count (a recipe id, then one or more build ids). The fixtures read it as
  at least three fields.
- A3 passes over a line whose field count is not exactly its kind's, while T55 speaks of a registry
  line "with a trailing field the Mac does not know" kept in a descriptor.
- Only comment lines and `order` lines can reach 1,000 bytes: every other record is at most 593 bytes,
  and an order line is 19 + 14 bytes for each build. The cap is therefore tested with comment lines.
- A3 does not say what a Mac does with a whole file over 512 KiB.
